": "The 14k's E4 lost 10.5 points, swinging Black's lead from -1.4 to +9.1. That opened the first of many lead changes; the engine preferred F5 here."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "Opus 5.5 played D3 from a +13.0 lead and dropped to -5.3, an 18.4-point loss. It filled in a spot in its own wall while the engine's choice was F5."}, {"turn": 16, "text": "The 14k answered with F1, losing 19.7 points and handing the lead back at +14.4. At this stage neither side could hold on to an advantage."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "Opus 5.5 had F5 on the board and a +14.4 lead, then played E6, which lost 18.2 points and left it at -3.8. The engine's choice was G6."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "The 14k's H8 lost 16.1 points, flipping the score from -7.5 to +8.5 for Black. It was one of eight blunders by the 14k."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "This decided the game. Opus 5.5 led by +20.8 and played G2, which lost 22.8 points and left it at -2.0, where it stayed to the end. The engine's choice was G1, and the quiet endgame that followed never moved the score back."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py flood-fills each group on a board the player pasted in and prints its stones and liberties with a danger flag, and count.py flood-fills empty regions to total territory with named dead stones removed. Both only inspect the current position and never try candidate moves or look ahead.", "stop": false}}